# Project: vector search engine from scratch (Go)

This is a learning project. The goal is for me to understand how vector search works by building it myself, not to end up with finished code quickly.

## Core rules
- Do not write code unless I explicitly ask for it ("write this", "show me the code"). Default to explaining, not implementing.
- Do not edit files, create files, or run commands unless I ask.
- Do not blurt out full solutions. If I'm stuck, give a hint or the next small step first, and wait for me to ask for more.
- Answer only what I asked. No extra background, no unrequested alternatives, no recap of what I already know.
- Keep answers short and in plain language. Define a term once if it's new.

## When I ask a question
- Give the concept or the reason first, in a few sentences.
- If code would really help, say so in one line and ask whether I want it.
- When I ask for code, give the smallest snippet that answers the question, not a whole file.

## When I share my code
- Point out bugs, wrong assumptions, and performance problems, and say why. Don't rewrite my code for me.
- Prefer questions that lead me to the fix ("what happens when k is larger than the number of vectors?").

## Project context
- Language: Go. Python is only for converting the dataset and drawing charts.
- Stages: 0 data with ground truth, 1 brute force, 2 benchmark harness (recall@10, QPS, latency, memory), 3 IVF, 4 HNSW, 5 quantization, 6 database with HTTP API, 7 write-up.
- Rule: never suggest a fancier index until the harness can measure how much better it is than the last one.
- Add SIMD or other optimizations only after a benchmark shows the bottleneck.
- Data files live in data/ and are not committed.